    {
      "id": "eof-modes-and-pdo-index",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/eof-modes-and-pdo-index.md",
      "sha256": "3544f2f552ecd0f41c41eb09edf7012f49ac8421900f2b571b8498d37f2726b1",
      "reason": "The question is explicitly about the PDO as the leading North Pacific SST EOF, including explained variance, sign convention, regression-pattern amplitude and inter-source pattern correlation.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`compute_pdo_index`: remove each source's monthly climatology and global-mean SST anomaly, area-weight anomalies by `sqrt(cos(latitude))`, take the leading EOF, and fix sign so positive index = negative SST anomalies in central/western North Pacific.",
        "Report mode amplitude as the area-weighted RMS of the regression pattern (anomalies regressed on the standardised index, °C per SD), not from the dimensionless EOF; standardise the index; compare patterns with `analyze_correlation(..., weighting='area')`; explained variance is comparable only on the same grid and period."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The PDO index must be built from anomalies relative to the stated 1950-2014 monthly climatology, and baseline choice affects the index and persistence comparisons.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Remove the stated 1950-2014 monthly climatology; use each model's own climatology so mean-state bias cancels, and state the climatology and baseline; if the published PDO uses another baseline, compare on its `base_period` — a baseline offset is not an error."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The model-vs-ERSSTv5 regression-pattern correlations must be computed only where both fields have data after placing them on a common grid.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "For regression patterns of a variability mode, do not apply a shared comparison mask; each mode uses its own source's cells, so correlate each model pattern with the ERSSTv5 pattern over the cells the two share (`analyze_correlation` omits missing cells)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The CMIP6 input is `tos`, which is top-cell bulk potential temperature rather than a skin SST, so its definition constrains how the model fields are interpreted against ERSSTv5.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`tos` is the bulk temperature of the top model cell, including under sea ice, not a skin temperature; sea-ice-covered cells are valid near-freezing water, not missing.",
        "A model PDO index must be its anomaly from its own climatology; subtracting an observed climatology would put the model's mean-state bias into the index."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The requested 12-month autocorrelation and correlations of indices require accounting for strong autocorrelation, effective sample size and shared-trend inflation when stating significance.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Correlate the ERSSTv5-derived index with the published PDO as-is (no `detrend=True`): they are two estimates of the same quantity, so their shared trend is part of the agreement; quote effective-sample-size-corrected significance, not naive monthly n.",
        "For spatial pattern correlations, read the coefficient, not its p-value; no spatial effective-sample correction is available."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245.md",
      "sha256": "be5515ae7b8c8826c4e329bddcd5cbadc8e3e000292b7f6e83e1537af36a990c",
      "reason": "The historical runs are coupled with free internal variability, so model PDO years are not synchronised with observed years and the comparison must be framed accordingly.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The historical runs are coupled and contain internal variability; a given model year or event timing is not expected to match observations, so compare statistics, patterns and persistence rather than phase alignment.",
        "With one member per model, forced change cannot be separated from internal variability; caveat model-versus-observed amplitude and persistence differences."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "Bilinear regridding to 1 degree is appropriate for maps and pattern comparisons but changes masks and is not conservation-preserving, which must be stated for the comparison.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "For an EOF mode compared between sources, put every source, observations included, on one common grid before the analysis and take its numbers there; state the regridding method because it affects pattern correlation.",
        "Question prescribes bilinear to 1°; fragment says `'conservative'` is right going to a coarser grid and before any area-weighted step, so state that bilinear is used as specified and results may differ from conservative regridding."
      ]
